WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.000
Hello to everyone, I'm Massimiliano Schettino and today we will follow a lesson on Test Automation topic, an important talk for word.

00:11.000 --> 00:20.000
I know there could be someone who might not like coding activities and who probably will never automate a test in his working life.

00:20.000 --> 00:30.000
But I can assure you that Test Automation is becoming even more important and that learning its basic could help you a lot in future.

00:30.000 --> 00:33.000
Let's start.

00:33.000 --> 00:40.000
As you can see in this first summary, we will start with an introduction on the Test Automation concept,

00:40.000 --> 00:49.000
focusing our attention on the reasons why it is spreading in automatic files and on its pros and cons, if there are any.

00:49.000 --> 00:55.000
Then we will make an overview of some of the most used tools for automation purposes.

00:55.000 --> 01:02.000
Cano with the integration of CAPL, Vtest Studio and DCU Test.

01:02.000 --> 01:09.000
The automation testing is the process of using tools, scripts and softwares to perform test cases.

01:09.000 --> 01:17.000
Test Automation focuses on replacing manual-like human activity with systems or devices.

01:17.000 --> 01:23.000
But why we should prefer it to manual testing? Let's see.

01:23.000 --> 01:29.000
As you probably know, the main validation activities can be summarized with this graphic.

01:29.000 --> 01:39.000
The starting point is usually a requirement list, the test to be analyzed in order to understand how the device under test should work.

01:39.000 --> 01:47.000
Using requirement as reference, test cases have to be written, so that they can be performed.

01:47.000 --> 01:58.000
Finally, a report has to be compiled and analyzed to find possible errors in the UET behavior or in the test execution.

01:58.000 --> 02:01.000
But this is not the end of the process.

02:01.000 --> 02:07.000
If you are testing an ECU software, for example, a new release will be periodically available for testing.

02:07.000 --> 02:13.000
Again, for a single software release, we could perform the same tests multiple times,

02:13.000 --> 02:23.000
or because there is the need to consider different conditions, or because we realize it has been previously done in a wrong way.

02:23.000 --> 02:27.000
So, steps 3 and 4 will be cyclically repeated.

02:27.000 --> 02:33.000
Let's now introduce test automation in this workflow.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
With the automation, the first step does not change.

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
It continues to be done manually.

02:39.000 --> 02:42.000
But we can now split the second step in two parts.

02:42.000 --> 02:48.000
With test writing, I still mean writing tests in a human understandable language,

02:48.000 --> 02:55.000
with proper tools like EBM doors or with more generic ones like Excel.

02:55.000 --> 03:02.000
With test automation, instead, I am referring to writing automatic tests with code or programming language,

03:02.000 --> 03:06.000
in one of the tools I was mentioning before.

03:06.000 --> 03:10.000
If requirements are clear enough and well-structured,

03:10.000 --> 03:16.000
the first sub-step can also be avoided and tests can be directly automated.

03:16.000 --> 03:20.000
These automatic tests have then to be validated,

03:20.000 --> 03:26.000
in order to be sure that they have been correctly implemented and so they are ready to be executed.

03:26.000 --> 03:29.000
At this point, most of the work is done.

03:29.000 --> 03:35.000
You have only to perform the tests and analyze the report automatically generated by the tool.

03:35.000 --> 03:40.000
So, we have essentially moved the focus from step 3,

03:40.000 --> 03:45.000
that is the core of the manual validation activity, to step 2, the test automation.

03:45.000 --> 03:49.000
But let's see what we have gained.

03:49.000 --> 03:54.000
First of all, it is a very reliable method.

03:54.000 --> 03:58.000
Human-generated error is reduced to automatic test generation.

03:58.000 --> 04:00.000
Then, it is fast.

04:00.000 --> 04:08.000
Obviously, a modern laptop can execute an operation sequence much faster than a human can.

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
And it is also repeatable.

04:10.000 --> 04:13.000
Automatic tests have to be written just once.

04:13.000 --> 04:17.000
Then, they can be executed for each software release.

04:17.000 --> 04:20.000
But what if it is the project to change?

04:20.000 --> 04:22.000
Again, no problem.

04:22.000 --> 04:27.000
Since usually, doing few changes to automatic tests is enough to adapt them.

04:27.000 --> 04:29.000
And so, it is versatile.

04:29.000 --> 04:39.000
As for the flexibility, test automation allows us to run tests not just during work hours, but also during the night.

04:39.000 --> 04:48.000
All these features increase a lot the number of tests runnable per day, making it a very efficient test method.

04:48.000 --> 04:57.000
All the characteristics reported in the previous slide lead to the probably best advantage in test automation.

04:57.000 --> 05:00.000
The reduction of the validation cost activity.

05:00.000 --> 05:11.000
But we have also to consider that automation allows to safely execute tests on hardware and loop that could be dangerous for a manual tester or for a vehicle.

05:11.000 --> 05:18.000
And the same applies to those tests that require conditions hard to reach for a human tester.

05:18.000 --> 05:21.000
As those relative to a very precise timing.

05:21.000 --> 05:27.000
In the end, automation testers do not have to worry about compiling reports.

05:27.000 --> 05:38.000
Usually, automation tools give as an output very detailed test reports at the end of each execution.

05:38.000 --> 05:41.000
Obviously, test automation has some limitations.

05:41.000 --> 05:50.000
There will always be some tests that cannot be automated or some others easier to perform manually than to be automated.

05:50.000 --> 06:13.000
And even if it allows an overall saving of time, human effort and money, at a certain point of validation, it requires a huge investment of these elements in order to buy and configure the needed hardware and or tool licenses and to write the automatic tests.

06:13.000 --> 06:28.000
So, if thanks to manual testing, it is possible to ensure an average of tests performed per day from day one, with automation validation times become more irregular.

06:28.000 --> 06:41.000
I could conclude this introduction inviting you to consider that they have simplified automation concept and that each project could require a different level of automation.

06:41.000 --> 06:57.000
It is possible to automate just a part of the test list, choosing the ones easier to be automated or the ones harder to be performed manually, so that manual execution can continue meanwhile test automation is going.

06:57.000 --> 07:04.000
Otherwise, manual testers could help automatically once in order to automate the entire list of tests.

07:04.000 --> 07:18.000
Again, automation can rise to a next level, in which a Python code translates written tests or even requirements in automatic tests, leaving to the tester the task of validating them.

07:18.000 --> 07:31.000
Test automation opens the door to a very wide range of combinations, where the only limits are testers' skill and project constraints.

07:31.000 --> 07:41.000
Let's see now how what we have talked about translates into practice analyzing three tools that allow us to automate tests.

07:41.000 --> 07:56.000
First, you should be able to find a dedicated lesson for each of them in the automotive training, so I will not explain in detail how to use them, since I just want to show the different approaches to automation that they offer.

07:56.000 --> 08:01.000
Let's start with the first one, CANU.

08:01.000 --> 08:09.000
From the previous lesson, you should know that CANU is a vector tool used for a very wide range of validation activities.

08:09.000 --> 08:19.000
One of its main features is that we can import all the databases you need, and each network will be simulated by default.

08:19.000 --> 08:34.000
CANU automatically creates a simulation for the messages of each ECU, with the information defined in the database, but obviously it is not going to simulate the logic which is behind each signal.

08:34.000 --> 08:46.000
We can easily modify the signal values thanks to node panel, but always in a manual test condition.

08:46.000 --> 08:52.000
The switch from manual to automation testing is made possible by CAPL scripts.

08:52.000 --> 09:02.000
First of all, because by editing network nodes, we can simulate the ECU's behavior, or more probably the part of it that we need for our tests.

09:02.000 --> 09:11.000
But we can also add a CAPL test module, useful to create automatic tests and to generate reports.

09:11.000 --> 09:14.000
Let's see both of them more in detail.

09:14.000 --> 09:22.000
Network nodes allows us to implement a wide range of functionalities.

09:22.000 --> 09:24.000
ECU software simulation.

09:24.000 --> 09:32.000
Essentially, we can define the conditions in which messages are or not sent, and in which signal values change.

09:32.000 --> 09:42.000
Panel control, we can create complex systems by establishing what happens when the system variables connected to the panel elements change their values.

09:42.000 --> 09:53.000
Heal control, we can implement a control strategy for hardware-in-the-loop instrumentation connected to the laptop where CANON measurement is running, and others.

09:53.000 --> 09:58.000
In this slide, you can see two examples.

09:58.000 --> 10:05.000
The first one, top left, is used in order to activate heel instrumentation when a measurement starts,

10:05.000 --> 10:18.000
and the second one, bottom right, is needed to simulate the signal that the easy use of an electric vehicle sent to the onboard charger in order to start the charging process.

10:18.000 --> 10:31.000
CAPL test modules, instead, contain all the test cases, functions, and variables useful for the test execution and reward generation.

10:31.000 --> 10:38.000
This is an example on the usage of diagnostic functions in CAPL test modules.

10:38.000 --> 10:45.000
In include section, we can add libraries.

10:45.000 --> 10:53.000
In this case, sim.diag.esu is a CAPL script containing functions called in the test cases.

10:53.000 --> 11:01.000
In variable brackets, we will find global variables we can use in the entire test module.

11:01.000 --> 11:10.000
Main test is the only block executed when start command is given in CANOTEST tab.

11:10.000 --> 11:14.000
All the test cases to be performed have to be listed here.

11:14.000 --> 11:20.000
Outside this block, we can define functions and test cases.

11:20.000 --> 11:27.000
Test case 1, for example, starts with the definition of local variables.

11:27.000 --> 11:35.000
That can be used in this test only, and then it continues with the test step.

11:35.000 --> 11:41.000
It has blocks in which the test cases is divided for other reasons.

11:41.000 --> 11:56.000
For each test step, we can add information in the test report, execute actions, wait for some events, and define the pass and fail conditions.

11:56.000 --> 12:10.000
Then we will pass to the next step, so to the next test case or function, and that's on, until the completion of the test unit.

12:10.000 --> 12:19.000
Once the code has been compiled and executed, CANOT will auto-generate a test report with a structure similar to this one.

12:19.000 --> 12:37.000
Here, we can find time sum and the result of each test step, plus each info we have requested in the code, like diagnostic services sent, diagnostic responses, comments, signal values, and so on.

12:37.000 --> 12:44.000
Vtest Studio is a vector-powerful development environment for creating automated test designs.

12:44.000 --> 12:50.000
To increase the efficiency and to simplify the reliability of test designs,

12:50.000 --> 12:58.000
Vtest Studio provides either programming-based, table-based, and graphical test notations and test development methods.

12:58.000 --> 13:02.000
Let's see them one by one.

13:02.000 --> 13:07.000
Test table editor is the main Vtest Studio automation method.

13:07.000 --> 13:12.000
Each test is symbolized by a folder containing a sequence of instructions.

13:12.000 --> 13:20.000
It has a list of predefined test commands in CAPL code that will be sequentially executed.

13:20.000 --> 13:35.000
In the slide, we can recognize, for example, a set to define a symbol value, a weight for a certain time, and a check in order to read a symbol value and to compare it with the target.

13:35.000 --> 13:45.000
In order to use them, we have just to drag and drop them inside the test and to parameterize them as we prefer.

13:45.000 --> 13:55.000
Direct calls to CAPL and C code portions and functions are available in order to execute any action not covered by these predefined commands.

13:55.000 --> 14:10.000
In the same view, we will find also a list of tests, one of user functions, and another with the symbols, so network signals and system variables.

14:10.000 --> 14:23.000
If we need to test a not-too-complicated logic, we can obtain a clear and concise representation of the same, thanks to test sequence diagram.

14:23.000 --> 14:36.000
Each graphical element can be added again by dragging and dropping them out of a toolbox, and the test case for each path through the diagram will be automatically generated.

14:38.000 --> 14:43.000
Vtest Studio offers also a trade-off between the previous two methods.

14:43.000 --> 14:59.000
Set diagram has both test table editor elements, access to symbols, predefined commands, user-defined function, and test sequence diagram once, as graphic view and automatic test generation.

14:59.000 --> 15:04.000
As it is easy to see, it is perfect for set machine tests.

15:04.000 --> 15:17.000
In the end, there is also the possibility to open the CAPL programming editor, whose explanation is behind the scope of this lesson.

15:17.000 --> 15:23.000
As you have probably realized by now, there is a strong link between Vtest Studio and Kano.

15:23.000 --> 15:33.000
In fact, you can import Kano configuration inside Vtest Studio in order to use in both tools the same variables and network signals.

15:33.000 --> 15:45.000
In Kano is it also possible to add a test configuration, a panel with which to choose Vtest Studio tests and to run them while measurement is ongoing.

15:48.000 --> 15:53.000
Once the execution is finished, from the same panel it is possible to open the test report.

15:53.000 --> 16:04.000
It has the same structure of CAPL test module 1, with the difference that we have not to indicate during test implementation what we need inside the report,

16:04.000 --> 16:10.000
since it will be automatically filled with all statistics, results, and step details.

16:10.000 --> 16:14.000
Obviously, we can also add some comments or additional info.

16:14.000 --> 16:19.000
The last tool I want to tell you about is ECU-Test.

16:19.000 --> 16:24.000
It is not as known as vectors tools, but it is not less powerful.

16:24.000 --> 16:34.000
ECU-Test is a software tool developed by TestTronic for test and validation of embedded systems,

16:34.000 --> 16:39.000
and used as a standard tool in the development of automotive ECUs.

16:39.000 --> 16:44.000
It allows to automate the control of the whole test environment,

16:44.000 --> 16:49.000
supports a broad range of test tools, and facilitates each validation step.

16:50.000 --> 16:54.000
ECU-Test is based on very user-friendly commands and interfaces,

16:54.000 --> 17:00.000
does not require programming skills, and allows to use shared libraries.

17:02.000 --> 17:08.000
In this image, you can identify a common software development and integration workflow.

17:08.000 --> 17:14.000
The starting point is the requirement, from which the software or the function is developed.

17:15.000 --> 17:21.000
The software model is used as base in order to generate the code creating a software code.

17:22.000 --> 17:27.000
Individual codes are then integrated to obtain the software code of a control unit,

17:27.000 --> 17:33.000
and, connecting together all these use, a first prototype of our vehicle will be ready.

17:33.000 --> 17:39.000
The task of the validation team is starting from a test specification document,

17:39.000 --> 17:44.000
to generate and execute tests for each of the steps just listed.

17:45.000 --> 17:50.000
And the first powerful ECU-Test feature is the possibility to cover all of them.

17:50.000 --> 17:54.000
And I will tell you more.

17:55.000 --> 17:58.000
It can be done using a single automatic test.

17:59.000 --> 18:03.000
Thanks the possibility to connect to all necessary validation tools, in fact,

18:04.000 --> 18:09.000
ECU-Test allows us to test the same functionality, either in modeling a loop,

18:09.000 --> 18:14.000
in order to test the controller logic on the simulated model of the plant,

18:14.000 --> 18:19.000
or in software-in-loop, where you test code generated from the model,

18:20.000 --> 18:26.000
or in hardware-in-loop, in which one or just few ECUs are tested in a simulated environment,

18:27.000 --> 18:31.000
or in vehicle-in-loop system, to test the entire vehicle.

18:31.000 --> 18:40.000
Here we can see the main possible scenarios that may arise during a project and that could stamp validation team.

18:41.000 --> 18:47.000
It happens often enough that a different software version is realized,

18:48.000 --> 18:52.000
and so signal names, calibrations, and model parameters will change.

18:52.000 --> 18:58.000
But it may also happen that it is the ECU version to be modified.

18:59.000 --> 19:02.000
In this case, we should deal with differences in the pinout,

19:03.000 --> 19:09.000
pins added or deleted, pins numbers changed, new sensor and or activators, and so on.

19:10.000 --> 19:14.000
Again, it is not so rare that a different heel version is chosen.

19:15.000 --> 19:20.000
Variation tools, plant model, and network may be subject to change.

19:20.000 --> 19:26.000
In the end, the worst case is the decision to modify all test bench technologies,

19:27.000 --> 19:34.000
and so not only tools and connection will change, but IO and the entire logic behind our system too.

19:35.000 --> 19:41.000
A second ECU test strength is the possibility to cope all these changes

19:42.000 --> 19:44.000
without modifying all the test cases already written.

19:44.000 --> 19:50.000
This is connected to another ECU characteristic.

19:51.000 --> 19:58.000
It has the chance to connect together a wide range of validation tools and test benches,

19:59.000 --> 20:02.000
regardless of the company, and to make them cooperate.

20:03.000 --> 20:08.000
This avoids any kind of limitation in the choice of a software or hardware component

20:08.000 --> 20:11.000
different from the one used until a certain point.

20:14.000 --> 20:19.000
Tracetronix was able to create a tool with all these unviable features

20:20.000 --> 20:25.000
thanks to a very particular and stratified structure, summarized in this image.

20:25.000 --> 20:37.000
On the left, we can see the system under test that communicates with one of the test environments we saw in the previous slides.

20:38.000 --> 20:44.000
ECU test is able to interact both with the system and the test bench thanks to specific ports.

20:44.000 --> 20:48.000
There are two files that make this possible.

20:49.000 --> 20:58.000
The first one, called test configuration, contains the description of the environment, DBC, TCF, and the test settings.

20:59.000 --> 21:07.000
The second one, test bench configuration, contains the list of the validation tools ECU tests can connect to and their relative setups.

21:07.000 --> 21:17.000
As for test cases, in ECU tests they are represented by elements called packages and collected and organized in projects.

21:18.000 --> 21:26.000
Each package is composed by test steps, that can be atomic actions, wait, read, write, or sub-packages.

21:27.000 --> 21:30.000
It has links to another package, like functions.

21:30.000 --> 21:43.000
It is important to underline that each action we want to execute is not performed by ECU test itself, but it is just a request to a connected tool or to the system in loop.

21:44.000 --> 21:52.000
ECU test is not able to read a canned signal, but it can ask Kanu, for example, to read it and return its value.

21:53.000 --> 21:59.000
In same way, ECU test can request a signal recording and then a trace analysis.

22:00.000 --> 22:06.000
After each package or project execution, a test report will always be generated and presented.

22:09.000 --> 22:14.000
The slides show, in a better way, the possibilities offered by test configuration,

22:15.000 --> 22:24.000
that managing databases, health simulation models, test reports, settings, and so on, makes ECU test independent from the environment.

22:24.000 --> 22:33.000
And the test bench configuration, that allows ECU test to communicate with a wide range of hardware and software components.

22:34.000 --> 22:39.000
This is ECU test view when a package is open.

22:40.000 --> 22:46.000
Top left, you can see global mapping window, another of the elements that make this tool so interesting.

22:46.000 --> 22:58.000
Instead of using a network signal or an internal variable as it is, we can add it to a global mapping and insert this new object in the test.

22:58.000 --> 23:12.000
So, if for one of the already explained reasons, any of these elements should change, it will be enough to modify the relative global mapping object and all the involved tests will automatically update.

23:12.000 --> 23:21.000
Bottom left, we have the action tab, in which we can find all the elements we could need in our tests.

23:22.000 --> 23:28.000
As predefined functions, bus signals, diagnostic requests, fault injection elements, and so on.

23:28.000 --> 23:36.000
In order to use them, we have just to drag and drop them in the right part, where we find the editor panel.

23:37.000 --> 23:49.000
This is the package and so the test, structured as a sequence of consecutive test steps, like variable write, weight, signal read, conditional instructions, and so on.

23:49.000 --> 24:00.000
In the end, the bottom right part shows the list of local variables we can create and use in our test.

24:01.000 --> 24:05.000
Once the package has been completed and compiled, it can be executed.

24:06.000 --> 24:10.000
ECU test will show each test step result in real time.

24:10.000 --> 24:18.000
At the end, a report like this will be auto-generated and showed to the tester.

24:19.000 --> 24:27.000
As you can see, it is not so full of details, such as Vtest Studio One, but for sure it is more clean and clear.

24:27.000 --> 24:39.000
I would like to conclude this lesson with a comparative table between the three tools we have seen together.

24:40.000 --> 24:46.000
Let's start with the configurations we have to set before we start implementing our tests.

24:47.000 --> 24:52.000
For CAPL and Vtest Studio, we just need to configure the Canon project.

24:52.000 --> 25:00.000
While ECU test, that is connected to several tools, needs to configure all of them and ECU test itself.

25:01.000 --> 25:04.000
That though, we implement our tests.

25:05.000 --> 25:09.000
If you remember, with CAPL we have to write instructions line by line,

25:10.000 --> 25:16.000
while the other tools make available predefined functions to be dragged and dropped and to be parameterized.

25:16.000 --> 25:23.000
For this reason, CAPL requires a programming skill directly proportional to test complexity.

25:24.000 --> 25:31.000
Vtest Studio a limited one, cause we can still add CAPL lines, and ECU test does not need it.

25:33.000 --> 25:41.000
With CAPL, we have to write a line for each info we want to read in the test report, making it totally customizable.

25:41.000 --> 25:51.000
We cannot say the same for the other two, but if Vtest Studio report is very rich in details, ECU test 1 is more simple and intuitive.

25:52.000 --> 25:59.000
The last voice is the robustness against change of signals, variables, buses, and so on.

26:00.000 --> 26:04.000
Vector tools require a rework of the already implemented test.

26:04.000 --> 26:11.000
Unlike ECU test, that has several features that, if correctly used, can save a lot of time.

26:14.000 --> 26:16.000
I hope you all enjoyed my lesson.

26:17.000 --> 26:21.000
For any doubt or question, here you can find my email.

26:22.000 --> 26:25.000
I can only say thank you for your attention and goodbye.

26:25.000 --> 26:26.000
Thank you for your attention and goodbye.

